frankensim.bar-card.v1
# Marimba B4 over La Favre's B4 tube, its stop set to sit the tube on this bar; everything else as the rendered B4 card on the three-bar loss fit.
name	marimba-b4-fit3-tubetuned-rosewood-rubber
source	Bar outline: J. La Favre, lafavre.us/marimba-bars.htm, table of an unnamed commercial 5-octave marimba with rosewood bars: B row, length 12 9/16 in, width 1 7/8 in, thickness 0.91 in. Tube: the same page, La Favre's own padouk marimba, B4 resonator: aluminium tubing, outer diameter 2 1/4 in, wall 1/16 in, air column 5 3/4 in at 72 F, straight, 11/16 in below the bottom of the bar. Wood: A. Chaigne and V. Doutaut, Numerical simulations of xylophones. I, J. Acoust. Soc. Am. 101(1), 539-557 (1997), Table I p.544, Rosewood bar: E 2.13e10, density 1015, eta 2.35e-7 s, gamma_B 51.58 1/s, through the packs named below (E and density are read from the pack of Doutaut and Chaigne 1994, which prints the same values). Ratios: USDA Wood Handbook FPL-GTR-282, Honduras mahogany, stand-in. Mallet: the same Table I, Korogi No. 4 rubber, K 3.7e7, 23.6 g, measured on a padouk bar; 1.0 m/s and strike 15.9 cm of 29.3 cm as the paper's Fig. 8.
note	NOT in the source: the tube's stop. La Favre prints an air column of 5 3/4 in (146.050 mm) at A4 = 442, and that column sits 101.4 c sharp of this bar, so this card sets the stop as a maker would, at 155.883 mm (9.83 mm further down), solved once on the passive column with the bar's reflection in it so its first resonance sits on this bar's f1 of 493.884 Hz; the tube's cross-section and gap are as printed and no recording enters. On the render it sits at 493.947 Hz, 0.06 Hz (0.2 c) above f1: the one solve held the bar's reflection at its value on the printed column, and this step is the marimba's largest. La Favre set each stopper by ear and says his figures aren't absolute. His total length of 8 11/16 in leaves 2 15/16 in below the printed stop, for the stopper assembly and tuning up to 85 F; the assembly's thickness isn't printed, so whether this stop can reach isn't known. NOT in the source either: the undercut (solved here in the engine on this card's 56 x 8 x 6 mesh, modes only, a circular arc, half reach 50.525 mm, middle 9.982 mm, so f1 sits at equal temperament and f2 = 4 f1, from the builder's statement for its own padouk bars on the same page; the rosewood table prints no arch length and no tuning). Strike fraction carried from the xylophone bar; no marimba strike is printed. Not the recording's bar or mallet: Iowa states no bar wood and no bar outline; the recording's file names a cord mallet or a roll, and a Korogi rubber xylophone mallet is not that mallet. Tube and bar come from two instruments: the bar from the unnamed commercial table, the tube from La Favre's own marimba, printed at A4 = 442 and 72 F, rendered here at A4 = 440 in the card's gas; no correction made. "Bottom of bar" is read as the plane of the uncut lower face, so the wood at the bar's middle is a further 13.1 mm from the mouth. The tube is a closed-open column of three odd modes, every one under this card's band that the open-end law takes (ka under 1.5), coupled through the bar's own boundary elements at each tube mode's frequency; its wall doesn't scatter, the mouth's sound doesn't reflect off the bar on its way out, and there are no neighbouring bars or frame. Its mouth, 54.0 mm across inside, is wider than this 47.6 mm bar. The loss is a fit through three printed rosewood bars, two of D. latifolia and one unidentified Dalbergia from Madagascar, tried after seeing the 7.3x decay gap on A3, with no recording number in it.
